Joe and I met in 1966 when we began our  intern  ships at the Massachusetts General  Hospital in internal medicine I had been  a medical student at the University of  Pennsylvania the nation's oldest medical  school and Joe had been a had graduated  from Southwestern Medical College in  Dallas Texas the nation's youngest  medical school that I had never heard of  I thought it was a Bible School but  within two days after we began our  internship we realized that we had  something in common we were both  interested in the fundamental basis of  disease not only in taking care of our  patients which was our first  responsibility but also trying to figure  out why this person had a heart attack  why this person had rheumatoid arthritis  why did this person have pneumonia and  it was that interest in the basis of  disease that created this friendship  that is now lasted since 1966 after our  residency's we went to the National  Institutes of Health where both of us  worked in with very exceptional  scientists Jo with Marshall nirenberg  and I with Earl Staten and two of the  best most brilliant scientists at NIH  but we also had clinical duties and  there we saw a patient that actually  determined the scientific course of the  rest of our lives so the patient that  Mike and I saw at the NIH was a little  girl the first patient with the disease  called familial hypercholesterolemia  also called homozygous familial  hypercholesterolemia rfh and her brother  actually had the same disorder and this  little girl has a very high cholesterol  her cholesterol is as high as 800 to 900  milligrams per deciliter the normal of  child of this age would be about a 120  milligrams per deciliter and most of  this high cholesterol was in a  lipoprotein called LDL which is known to  everyone is the bad form of cholesterol  and when LD  accumulates in the blood as in this  little girl  it deposits in the skin in these little  yellow lumps you see on the surface of  our skin that call xanthomas and it also  accumulates in the artery wall and  produces coronary heart disease and this  little girl had a heart attack  when she was 6 years of age now this  little girl had only elevated LDL she  had no high blood pressure she had no  diabetes she didn't smoke she didn't  have a Type A personality her only risk  factor for having a heart attack at age  6 was this high level of LDL so this is  one of the best examples of a disease  where we really know the cause and the  cause of the disease and this old girl  as I say increased level of her LDL and  so it would be important to understand  the basis of why her LDL is elevated and  also how it causes her disease  this is LDL the lipoprotein that build  up in the plasma of this young girl it's  a spherical particle with it it's about  the size of a small virus and each LDL  particle has 1,500 molecules of  cholesterol within its core and it's  surrounded by a polar coat of  phospholipid and a protein called a PO B  and LDL circulates in the blood on  average for about three days in humans  and eventually it leaves the blood  stream when we began our work no one  knew anything about what controlled the  level of LDL in the blood we were  interested in this disease because not  only did the homozygotes have problems  but the heterozygotes people who inherit  one copy of the abnormal gene have a  moderately elevated cholesterol and they  have heart attacks not at age six but at  age 60 and so if you ignore the  homozygotes the heterozygotes trance  with their disease in an autosomal  dominant fashion through the the  pedigree and so having a single copy of  the mutant gene no matter what it is  produced a disease now at the time there  were about 35 single gene diseases that  had been worked out and that the  defective gene product was known all 35  of those were enzymes mutations in  enzymes and all of them were autosomal  recessive that means that the  heterozygote had no symptoms but in in  the case of familial  hypercholesterolemia the heterozygote  had a disease and we thought that if we  could understand this disease it would  be the first dominant disease that could  be elucidated in humans and it might  teach us something about proteins that  are that are not enzymes we assume that  it was not going to be an enzyme and  that it would probably be some disorder  of regulation now Joe had promised his  professor at Southwestern Medical  College that he would return there after  his training and Joe and I talked about  this and Joe and we decided that I would  also go to Dallas and we went and when  we went to Dallas we would both try to  figure out what type of defect could  cause this high LDL level and the unique  approach that we took was to study the  metabolism of cholesterol in cultured  cells we obtained biopsies from children  with familial hypercholesterolemia and  compared them with normal cells and  studied the regulation of cholesterol  synthesis because we thought that this  would be some regulatory abnormality so  here's an experiment that Mike and I  carried out in 1973 and it's arguably  probably the most exciting experiment  that we've ever done and it really  hooked us on science and also taught us  a lesson that I'll come back to after  describing the experiment and so as Mike  pointed out we wanted to understand  what there was about the regulation and  cholesterol synthesis in in these  children with homozygous FH it was  different from normal and so at the time  it was known from studies in rats and  mice that when you fed one of these  small animals cholesterol you would  suppress cholesterol synthesis in the  liver there was a hint that there was a  regulatory system level and that the  liver was was known to be the most  important organ in the body for  controlling cholesterol but there was no  way that we could get liver biopsies  from patients so we chose fibroblast  which one could get skin fibroblasts and  this was sort of a heretical idea at the  time the one would study a fundamental  process that was going on in the liver  in a fiberglass but we did it anyway and  here's the experiment and the first  experiment we did worked and it was  really quite dramatic so what we did is  to to compare fibroblast grown in fetal  calf serum which contains LDL from a  normal subject and one of these children  with homozygous familial  hypercholesterolemia and you can see in  the red curve on the on the left that  the level of the cholesterol synthesis  was very low almost undetectable now as  a as a surrogate for cholesterol  synthesis we use the enzyme hmg-coa  reductase which had been known from  these animal studies to be a key rate  controlling enzyme in the cholesterol  biosynthetic pathway so we were able to  set up a assay for this enzyme and we  would have to incubating the cells with  this fetal calf serum that contained LDL  we would make extracts and do the  measurement so you can see that at zero  time the in the red curve the level was  very low and then when we removed the  serum and the LDL from the medial the  next 24 hours the enzyme activity in the  normal cells in red went up 50 to 104  and then when we only in the right panel  when we remove when we added LDL back  you can see that that very high level of  the enzyme h mg k co a reductase came  down to the the basal level when the LDL  was added back so this is a this in  itself was a very interesting experiment  in the normal because they told us that  there was a an effect of LDL cholesterol  and regulating the activity of this  enzyme that reflected the cholesterol  biosynthetic pathway and normals but the  that in itself was exciting but the real  dramatic result was when this result and  the normal was compared to the fh  homozygote shown in yellow and you can  see that at zero time and the presence  of a fetal calf serum and LDL the level  of the enzyme HMG co-reductase and  cholesterol synthesis was a hundred  times higher than in the normal and and  it didn't change no matter what we did  when when removed the LDL a stayed high  and when added LDL back it stayed high  so this was really dramatic it was  really all and now that was one of the  things that I think not only was it an  exciting result but it it sort of  addicted us to the fact that that we  were really interested in all future  experiments and seeing big changes we  don't usually see changes of a  hundredfold but at least it told us that  one had to have really a big change to  really work out something now the  simplest interpretation from this result  when Mike and I sat down and tried to to  deal with it after 24 hours of being all  on the wall and off the wall of  excitement we decided that the  simplistic interpretation was that the  fh homozygote cells had a mutation in  their enzyme HMG co-reductase  they rendered the enzyme resistant to  feedback regulation by cholesterol so we  said okay well if that's true then we  should be able to add cholesterol not in  LDL but in ethanol and we would get the  same result but then when we  did that the curve the yellow curve in  the fh homozygote was he came down from  this hundred fold just like the normal  and so the normal and the fh homozygote  elves are now responding to cholesterol  and ethanol  where's the fh homozygote did not  respond to the to the LDL so that then  told us that our original hypothesis was  wrong and now we have a new hypothesis  that said that the defect in the fh  homozygote was a failure to extract the  cholesterol from LDL so how does a  normal sell this now became the question  how does a normal cell extract the  cholesterol from LDL so we radio labeled  LDL with ID and 125 and incubated it  with the fibroblast and what we saw was  that when we incubated the L do with  normal fiberglass there was high  affinity cell surface binding that was  saturable is shown by the red line on  this slide but when we incubated the LDL  with the fh homozygote fibroblast there  was no binding so the problem was in the  end that there must have been a receptor  on the cell surface that binds LDL and  the fh homozygote cells lack this  receptor now this was 1974 it was in the  very very early days receptors were kind  of theoretical concepts how they worked  was there was very little known about  how they actually worked but we went one  step further when we incubated the cells  with I 125 LDL we followed what happened  to the LDL after the binding reaction  and we were shocked to find that the I  125 LDL was totally degraded the protein  was degraded down to amino acids and the  only way that could happen would be if  the LDL had been taken into the cell and  degraded in the digestive organelle of  the cell which is the lysosome so once  we knew that there was a putative  receptor on the  surface of the cells we advanced the  concept of an LDL receptor pathway in  which LDL bound to receptors on the cell  surface in specialized regions call Cody  pits and and our colleague and  collaborator Richard Anderson was able  to demonstrate by electron microscopy  the localization of a farad and labeled  LDL in these coated pits Cody pits  occupy only about 2% of the cell surface  of a fibroblast and and so it's a very  specialized region for rapid into  cytosis and internalization of of  proteins like LDL  so once the LDL bound to Cody pit the  Cody pits pinched soft foam Cody  vesicles which delivered the LDL to  induce ohms and then ultimately the  lysosomes where the LDL was degraded and  the cholesterol was released and that  cholesterol was in we used by the self a  membrane synthesis so we now knew from  the LDL receptor pathway that that in  addition to this to obtaining  cholesterol from cholesterol synthesis  cells could also obtain a pain  cholesterol through the receptor  mediated endocytosis of LDL in this  cholesterol and not only was used for  the synthesis of plasma membranes but  also it exerted a feedback regulation on  on the enzymes involved in the  cholesterol biosynthetic pathway and it  also exerted a feedback suppression on  the gene for the LDL receptor someone  had a coordinate a feedback regulatory  system based on both entry from LDL and  a biosynthesis so what happened in the  fh homozygote selves the lacked LDL  receptor how did they obtain their  cholesterol for membrane synthesis and  maintain cholesterol homeostasis so in  these cells they relaxed  virtually completely on the cholesterol  biosynthetic pathway and and that is  their source of  cholesterol and butt though so the cell  is happy because the cell has its  cholesterol for its membrane synthesis  but it's all at the expense of an  elevated LDL in the bloodstream which is  the root of this disease familial  hypercholesterolemia when the LDL builds  up and ultimately deposits in the skin  for these anthems and in the artery wall  to produce atherosclerosis so over the  next few years we were able to define in  detail the pathway by which LDL delivers  the cholesterol to cells and it's shown  on this slide  after binding to the receptor encoded  pits the LDL is internalized and it's  delivered to light to endosomes and  within endosomes there are proton pumps  that acidify the interior of the  endosome that causes a conformational  change in the receptor so that it  releases the LDL particle the LDL is  then carried further into lysosomes  where it's totally degraded and the  cholesterol is liberated for use in to  make new membranes LDL derived  cholesterol is also used for steroid  hormones bile acids it's used to make  new lipoproteins and it as Joe mentioned  it exerts regulatory actions so that LDL  derived cholesterol is really important  biologically after the LDL dissociates  from the receptor the receptor returns  to the cell surface in a recycling  endosome and each of these each LDL  receptor makes one trip into and out of  the cell every 20 minutes and it makes  hundreds of trips during its lifetime  and each time it carries an LDL particle  with 1,500 molecules of cholesterol  ester we gave this process its general  name which is receptor mediated  endocytosis and after we had described  the system for LDL it turned out that  other scientists found that other  proteins bound to their own receptors on  the surface but they were taken in and  into the cell in the same way  as LDL and the examples include hormones  such as insulin transport proteins such  as transferrin which transports iron and  vitamin and the protein that transports  vitamin b12 so receptor mediated  endocytosis turned out to be a general  property of all cells at least all  nucleated cells and it's interesting  that we had no idea that we would  stumble into something that was so  general as all we wanted to do is figure  out what was wrong with these children  with FH so you know this slide shows  what we look like when we started and  what we look like now I think it's  important for any young people looking  at this video not to picture us the way  we are now as shown on the right but  picture us as we were in 1972 when I was  31 years old and Joe was 32 years old  that's when we made these big  discoveries and and the thrill of  discovery when you're young and working  hard and something happens like that FH  experiment that Joe described the thrill  of that is amazing and I want to say one  other thing that it's a fantastic thing  to share that experience with somebody  else we've we have the longest running  partnership and we think in the history  of science to be honest and and I I just  have a hard time understanding why  somebody would want to do science  totally on their own without somebody to  share the adventure you you wouldn't  want to take a trip to Paris by yourself  would you I mean you you want somebody  along who can share the excitement of  that adventure and that and that's what  Joe and I have had so it's been it's a  fantastic thing to have a partnership in  science  you
 

